#: ../../operations.rst:840
msgid ""
"A name on demand is a host name. A wildcard and an IP address are never "
"obtained on demand. The issuer follows the name as for a listed name: a "
"public name goes to the ACME authority, and an internal name such as "
"``app.localhost`` goes to the local authority, which answers at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:842
msgid ""
"**What a client sees.** A handshake for a name that a guard refuses does "
"not wait. It gets what any unknown name gets: the first certificate of "
"``crt`` when the frontend has one, and a TLS alert otherwise. A handshake "
"whose order takes longer than ``wait`` gets the same, and the order goes "
"on, so a later handshake finds the certificate. One order runs at a time, "
"so many new names at once are served one after the other. HTTP/3 never "
"waits: a QUIC handshake uses the certificate once a TCP handshake has "
"obtained it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:844
msgid ""
"**Keeping and releasing.** A name on demand has a line in ``show certs`` "
"with ``on-demand`` in the column ``origin``, and it is renewed in the "
"background like a listed name. At its renewal Sibuna asks two questions. "
"Did a handshake name it since the certificate was obtained? Does ``ask`` "
"still allow it? If either answer is no, the name is released: it is no "
"longer served or renewed, and its line leaves ``show certs``. The log "
"says which. A name whose first order fails is released at once and "
"refused until its retry delay has passed. The next handshake after that "
"asks again. A restart forgets the names and keeps the certificates: the "
"first handshake for each loads its certificate from the storage, with no "
"order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:846
msgid ""
"**More than one authority.** An authority can be down, or can refuse us "
"for a while. We name a second one, and Sibuna asks it in the same attempt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:848
msgid ""
"fn edge() -> Map<Value> {\n"
"    return {\n"
"        global: { acme: {\n"
"            email: \"ops@example.org\",\n"
"            fallback: [\n"
"                {\n"
"                    directory: \"https://acme.zerossl.com/v2/DV90\",\n"
"                    email: \"ops@example.org\",\n"
"                    eab: { kid: \"kid-from-the-dashboard\", key_file: "
"\"/etc/sibuna/zerossl.key\" },\n"
"                },\n"
"            ],\n"
"        } },\n"
"        frontends: {\n"
"            web: { binds: [\"0.0.0.0:443\"], tls: { auto: "
"[\"example.org\"] } },\n"
"            plain: { binds: [\"0.0.0.0:80\"] },\n"
"        },\n"
"        backends: { app: { servers: [\"10.0.0.1:80\"] } },\n"
"    }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:870
msgid ""
"The keys ``directory``, ``email``, ``ca_file`` and ``eab`` of "
"``global.acme`` describe the first authority, here Let's Encrypt by "
"default. Each member of ``fallback`` has the same four keys, and "
"``directory`` is required there. One attempt for a name asks the "
"authorities in order and stops at the first certificate. The log names "
"each authority that failed and the one that is asked next. When all fail, "
"the attempt fails once and is tried again after the usual delay, with the "
"first authority first. A certificate is stored under the authority that "
"issued it, and at a start we serve the stored certificate that expires "
"last. ``show certs`` names the issuer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:872
msgid ""
"Some authorities issue only to an account that they know already. They "
"hand out a key identifier and a MAC key, the *external account binding*. "
"We write the identifier into ``kid`` and the key, in base64url as the "
"authority shows it, into a file that only the daemon's user can read. "
"Sibuna signs the registration with it once. The key is never logged. An "
"authority that wants a binding and gets none fails with ``ACME012`` "
"before we send it anything signed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:874
msgid ""
"All authorities share one account key, the one in the directory of the "
"first authority. So every node that shares the storage answers an HTTP-01 "
"challenge of any of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:876
msgid ""
"**When a certificate is renewed.** Without other knowledge we renew at "
"two thirds of the lifetime. An authority that supports renewal "
"information (RFC 9773) may know better: it spreads the load of its "
"clients, and it asks for an early renewal before it revokes a "
"certificate. Sibuna asks the authority for each certificate, every six "
"hours or as often as the authority says, between one hour and one day. "
"The answer is a window. We take one time in it, the same on every node "
"and after every restart, and ``show certs`` shows it in the column "
"``renews``. Two bounds are ours. We never renew later than four fifths of "
"the lifetime, whatever the authority says. And a certificate that we just "
"obtained is not renewed in its first two minutes. The new order tells the "
"authority which certificate it replaces. ``ari: false`` turns the "
"questions off. An authority without renewal information is not asked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:878
msgid ""
"**OCSP stapling.** Some authorities put the URL of an OCSP responder into "
"their certificates. For such a certificate Sibuna asks the responder "
"whether the certificate is still good, checks the signature of the answer "
"against the issuer, and staples the answer to every handshake. A client "
"then needs no connection to the responder. Sibuna asks again at the "
"middle of the validity of each answer, keeps the answer beside the "
"certificate, and staples it again after a restart. An answer whose "
"validity ends is no longer stapled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:880
msgid ""
"certificate installed --> ask the responder --> good     --> staple, ask "
"again at half of the validity\n"
"                                             --> revoked  --> staple "
"nothing, renew the certificate\n"
"                                             --> failure  --> serve "
"without a staple, ask again later"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:886
msgid ""
"When the responder says ``revoked``, Sibuna logs it and renews the "
"certificate at once. A certificate that it obtained a moment ago waits a "
"short time first, so a responder cannot cause a loop of orders. A "
"responder that fails changes nothing for the clients: the certificate is "
"served without a staple, the failure is logged with ``ACME013``, and the "
"question is repeated with growing delays. A certificate that names no "
"responder gets no staple, and nobody is asked. Let's Encrypt names none "
"since 2025. ``ocsp: false`` turns stapling off. Only ``http`` responders "
